<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">British Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as British</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The British Empire comprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, colonies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> and other territories ruled or administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the late sixteenth to the twentieth century. At its height it was the largest empire in history, covering about a quarter of the world's land. It conquered or absorbed many states covered by the site, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> polities, and decolonised mainly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/british-empire.jpg" alt="British Empire" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hoshie · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The empire began with the overseas possessions and trading posts that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> established in the late 16th and early 17th centuries, and with Scottish colonisation attempts in the 17th century. Motivated by the wealth of the Portuguese and Spanish empires, England, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> founded colonies and trade networks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The first permanent English settlement in the Americas was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, founded in 1607, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>, chartered in 1600, challenged the Portuguese monopoly of trade with Asia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wars of the 17th and 18th centuries with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the dominant colonial power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-subah">Mughal Bengal</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757 made it a major power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Britain lost the Thirteen Colonies after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, and independence was acknowledged at the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1783. Colonial expansion then turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the Pacific. The first convicts arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 1788, and colonisation brought disease, violence and dispossession to Indigenous Australians.</p><p>Parliament abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in the empire in 1807, and the Slavery Abolition Act ended slavery there from 1 August 1834, with an apprenticeship system that lasted until 1838 and compensation paid to slave-owners. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had no serious rival at sea, and between 1815 and 1914 around 26 million km2 and roughly 400 million people were added. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> was defeated in 1818, rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> became the Great Game, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a> followed.</p><p>The white settler colonies gained self-government step by step, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>'s confederation in 1867 to the Australian federation in 1901, and the term dominion status was introduced in 1907. The empire reached its largest extent after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, covering 24 per cent of the Earth's land in 1920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> gained independence on 15 August 1947 with the partition that cost hundreds of thousands of lives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> spoke of the wind of change in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> in 1960, and nearly thirty colonies became independent in that decade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Most former colonies joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth of Nations</a>, a voluntary association of equal members. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> was handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on 1 July 1997, an event that symbolised the end of the empire for many. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> keeps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over fourteen overseas territories. Historians' approaches to the empire are diverse and still changing, and post-colonial studies have led to a critical re-evaluation of the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “British Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British Raj ruled the Indian subcontinent as the centrepiece of the <b>British Empire</b> for nearly ninety years, building the railways, armies, laws and civil services inherited by South Asia’s modern states, while its economic policies, famines and communal politics fuelled the nationalist movements that ended it in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Canada was the first self-governing dominion of the <b>British Empire</b>, and its gradual, negotiated path from colony to independence, through responsible government, Confederation and the Statute of Westminster, became a model for Australia, New Zealand and South Africa and for the later transformation of the empire into the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Punakha (1910) then made Bhutan a de facto protectorate of the <b>British Empire</b>, whose protection guarded it from Tibet and Qing China; the friendship treaty with India of 1949 took over that role, and the border with China was closed in 1960 after an influx…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>English trading companies and colonies in North America, the Caribbean and India laid the foundations of the later <b>British Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Burmese expansion into Arakan, Assam and Manipur brought the kingdom into conflict with British India, and the resulting Anglo-Burmese wars led to the incorporation of Burma into the <b>British Empire</b>, with consequences that shaped the borders and ethnic politics of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rwanda joined the Commonwealth of Nations in 2009 as one of its few members with no historical link to the <b>British Empire</b>, and in 2008 it changed the medium of instruction in its schools from French to English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
